00:02:52,814 --> 00:02:58,770
Once upon a time in the quiet town of Newbite, there lived a curious teenager named Aria.

26
00:02:59,054 --> 00:03:03,680
She wasn't like most kids in her school, while others were busy with sports or music.

27
00:03:04,078 --> 00:03:09,520
Aria was fascinated by machines, especially the idea of making machines think like humans.

28
00:03:09,934 --> 00:03:16,400
Her curiosity began one evening when she asked her grandfather, who used to be a computer engineer, can machines ever think?

29
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:20,880
Her grandfather smiled and said, That's what artificial intelligence is all about.

30
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:22,560
Arya's eyes lit up.

31
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:24,320
Artificial intelligence?

32
00:03:24,654 --> 00:03:25,040
What's that?

33
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:27,440
So he began to tell her a story.

34
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:37,120
Not a fairy tale, but a real story about the science and ideas behind machines that learn, decide, and sometimes even surprise their creators.

35
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:46,160
Artificial intelligence, or AI is the science of making machines that can do things that normally require human intelligence.

36
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:53,920
This includes tasks like recognizing faces, understanding speech, making decisions, and even playing games.

37
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:55,920
But AI isn't magic.

38
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:59,360
It's built through programming, mathematics, and data.

39
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:04,090
Aria imagined a robot that could talk like a human and help with homework.

40
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:05,680
Her grandfather nodded.

41
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:08,640
That's one kind of AI, but there are many types.

42
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:11,840
He explained that AI isn't just about robots.

43
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:21,280
In fact, most AI systems are just computer programs running inside machines we already use, like phones, laptops, or even refrigerators.

44
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:27,360
Her grandfather told her that AI comes in two main types narrow AI and general AI.

45
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,160
Narrow AI is the kind we see today. It's designed to do one specific task.

46
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:39,040
For example, the AI in a smartphone that unlocks the screen by recognizing your face is only good at that one job.

47
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:41,200
It can't cook or write a story.

48
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,840
General AI, on the other hand, would be as smart as a human,

49
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:49,200
able to learn anything and do many tasks.

50
00:04:50,222 --> 00:04:52,388
But this type of AI doesn't exist yet.

51
00:04:52,942 --> 00:04:54,372
It's more of a dream for now.

52
00:04:55,110 --> 00:04:57,604
Aria asked, How do these machines learn?

53
00:04:57,966 --> 00:05:01,476
That's where machine learning comes in, her grandfather replied.

54
00:05:01,998 --> 00:05:07,812
Machine learning is a type of AI that learns from data instead of being told what to do step by step.

55
00:05:08,398 --> 00:05:10,212
Imagine teaching a dog to sit.

56
00:05:10,766 --> 00:05:13,508
You show it how, give it treats, and repeat.

57
00:05:14,062 --> 00:05:16,176
Over time, the dog learns.

58
00:05:16,558 --> 00:05:20,740
Machine learning works the same way, you feed it data and it finds patterns.

59
00:05:21,198 --> 00:05:27,300
For example, if you want a computer to recognize pictures of cats, you show it thousands of cat pictures.

60
00:05:27,822 --> 00:05:30,244
It starts to see what cats usually look like.

61
00:05:30,606 --> 00:05:33,284
Furry, whiskers, pointy ears.

62
00:05:33,838 --> 00:05:38,052
Over time, it learns to tell a cat apart from a dog or a chair.

63
00:05:38,574 --> 00:05:41,376
The program that does this learning is called a model.

64
00:05:41,870 --> 00:05:46,020
A model is like a brain built by the computer using the data it was given.

65
00:05:46,510 --> 00:05:49,056
The more data it gets, the better it learns.

66
00:05:49,422 --> 00:05:51,536
But how does the computer know what a cat is?

67
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:52,964
Aria asked.

68
00:05:53,358 --> 00:05:57,456
Her grandfather said, that's thanks to something called a neural network.

69
00:05:57,710 --> 00:06:02,336
It's a method used in machine learning that's inspired by how our brains work.

70
00:06:02,702 --> 00:06:06,852
A neural network is made up of layers of tiny parts called neurons.

71
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:10,724
These are not real brain cells, but math functions.

72
00:06:11,278 --> 00:06:17,252
Each neuron takes in numbers, does some math, and passes the result to the next layer of neurons.

73
00:06:17,838 --> 00:06:24,228
Imagine passing a note through a group of friends and each one adds or changes a word before giving it to the next.

74
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:28,388
By the end, the note may have transformed in a useful way.

75
00:06:28,942 --> 00:06:31,844
That's what a neural network does to data.

76
00:06:32,334 --> 00:06:37,284
It turns it into something meaningful, like recognizing a cat in a picture.

77
00:06:37,806 --> 00:06:42,724
The more layers a network has, the more complex patterns it can understand.

78
00:06:43,054 --> 00:06:46,404
When a network has many layers, it's called deep learning.

79
00:06:46,798 --> 00:06:50,084
To get a neural network to work, it needs to be trained.

80
00:06:50,478 --> 00:06:55,076
Training is the process where the model is shown lots of examples so it can learn.

81
00:06:55,534 --> 00:07:00,836
Training involves giving the model data and letting it guess something like whether a picture has a cat.

82
00:07:01,326 --> 00:07:06,564
At first it guessed badly, but then it compares its guess to the correct answer.

83
00:07:06,926 --> 00:07:11,616
If it's wrong, it adjusts itself using a method called backpropagation.

84
00:07:11,982 --> 00:07:14,948
Backpropagation is like checking your math homework.

85
00:07:15,310 --> 00:07:20,068
If the answer is wrong, you go back, find where you messed up, and fix it.

86
00:07:20,494 --> 00:07:23,972
In AI, this helps the model improve step by step.

87
00:07:24,366 --> 00:07:28,416
This cycle of guessing, checking, and adjusting is repeated many times.

88
00:07:28,782 --> 00:07:31,236
The model slowly gets better at the task.

89
00:07:31,790 --> 00:07:33,216
Can AI make mistakes?

90
00:07:33,614 --> 00:07:34,532
Arya asked.

91
00:07:35,054 --> 00:07:37,060
Oh yes, her grandfather said.

92
00:07:37,550 --> 00:07:40,196
AI is smart in some ways, but not perfect.

93
00:07:40,686 --> 00:07:42,724
AI only learns from the data we give it.

94
00:07:43,054 --> 00:07:45,860
If the data is bad, the AI will be bad.

95
00:07:46,350 --> 00:07:47,684
This is called bias.

96
00:07:48,014 --> 00:07:56,260
For example, if a face recognition system is trained mostly on photos of light skinned people, it might not work well on darker skinned people.

97
00:07:56,686 --> 00:07:59,716
Also, AI doesn't really understand the world.

98
00:08:00,078 --> 00:08:02,084
It only sees patterns in numbers.

99
00:08:02,542 --> 00:08:05,796
It doesn't know what a cat feels like or why we love them.

100
00:08:06,190 --> 00:08:12,740
That's why AI can sometimes be fooled by simple tricks, like weird images that a human would never mistake for a cat.

101
00:08:13,216 --> 00:08:16,256
AI is everywhere, her grandfather explained.

102
00:08:16,526 --> 00:08:27,012
It helps recommend videos on YouTube, powers voice assistants like Siri or Alexa, drives some cars, and even helps doctors find diseases and scans.

103
00:08:27,374 --> 00:08:29,536
But not all AI is harmless.

104
00:08:29,806 --> 00:08:38,016
It can be used for spying, spreading fake news, or making decisions that affect people's lives like who gets a loan or a job.

105
00:08:38,286 --> 00:08:45,233
That's why it's important for people to understand how AI works, so they can ask good questions and build it responsibly.

106
00:08:45,233 --> 00:08:48,736
Aria asked, Will AI take over the world?

107
00:08:49,134 --> 00:08:50,468
Her grandfather laughed.

108
00:08:50,830 --> 00:08:53,316
Not like in the movies, but it will change the world.

109
00:08:53,710 --> 00:08:56,976
The future of AI depends on how people choose to use it.

110
00:08:57,326 --> 00:09:03,172
It can help solve big problems like climate change or disease, but it also needs rules and careful thinking.

111
00:09:03,502 --> 00:09:07,588
Just like fire or electricity, AI is a tool, a powerful one.

112
00:09:07,918 --> 00:09:09,936
If used wisely, it can do great good.

113
00:09:10,410 --> 00:09:12,900
Aria sat back, her mind buzzing.

114
00:09:13,262 --> 00:09:15,972
She had started the day wondering if machines could think.

115
00:09:16,302 --> 00:09:23,652
Now she knows that while they don't think like humans, they can do amazing things through learning, data, and clever programming.

116
00:09:24,014 --> 00:09:27,456
She smiled and said, Maybe I'll build an AI someday.

117
00:09:27,694 --> 00:09:29,188
Her grandfather smiled too.

118
00:09:29,454 --> 00:09:35,492
Just remember, it's not about making a machine smart, it's about making it useful and fair for everyone.

119
00:09:35,918 --> 00:09:41,536
And from that day on, Aria started her journey not just to understand AI, but

120
00:09:42,336 --> 00:09:52,016
to shape it with care, creativity, and curiosity. We know humans learn from their past experiences, and machines follow instructions given by humans.

121
00:09:53,136 --> 00:10:03,216
But what if humans can train the machines to learn from their past data and do what humans can do and much faster? Well, that's called machine learning. But it's a lot more than just learning.

122
00:10:03,296 --> 00:10:13,296
It's also about understanding and reasoning. So today we will learn about the basics of machine learning. So that's Paul. He loves listening to new songs.

123
00:10:14,416 --> 00:10:28,336
He either likes them or dislikes them. Paul decides this on the basis of the songs tempo, genre, intensity, and the gender of voice. For simplicity, let's just use tempo and intensity for now.

