So here, tempo is on the x-axis ranging from relaxed to fast, whereas intensity is on the y axis, ranging from light to soaring.

125
00:10:38,336 --> 00:10:47,696
We see that Paul likes the song with fast tempo and soaring intensity, while he dislikes the song with relaxed tempo

126
00:10:47,936 --> 00:11:00,096
and light intensity. So now we know Paul's choices. Let's say Paul listens to a new song. Let's name it as song A. Song A has fast tempo and a soaring intensity. So it lies somewhere here.

127
00:11:00,336 --> 00:11:06,576
Looking at the data, can you guess whether Paul will like the song or not? Correct. So Paul likes this song.

128
00:11:06,736 --> 00:11:17,696
By looking at Paul's past choices, we were able to classify the unknown song very easily, right? Let's say now Paul listens to a new song. Let's label it as song B.

129
00:11:17,856 --> 00:11:30,016
So song B lies somewhere here with medium tempo and medium intensity. Neither relaxed nor fast, neither light nor soaring. Now can you guess whether Paul likes it or not?

130
00:11:30,176 --> 00:11:37,856
Not able to guess whether Paul will like it or dislike it? Are the choices unclear? Correct. We could easily classify song A.

131
00:11:38,016 --> 00:11:45,376
But when the choice became complicated, as in the case of song B, yes, and that's where machine learning comes in. Let's see how.

132
00:11:45,536 --> 00:11:54,656
In the same example for song B, if we draw a circle around the song B, we see that there are four votes for like, whereas one vote for dislike.

133
00:11:54,816 --> 00:12:03,550
If we go for the majority votes, we can say that Paul will definitely like the song. That's all. This was a basic machine learning algorithm also. It's called K nearest neighbors.

134
00:12:04,016 --> 00:12:16,656
So this is just a small example in one of the many machine learning algorithms. Quite easy, right? Believe me, it is. But what happens when the choices become complicated? As in the case of song B.

135
00:12:16,736 --> 00:12:24,736
That's when machine learning comes in. It learns the data, builds the prediction model, and when the new data point comes in, it can easily predict for it.

136
00:12:24,976 --> 00:12:36,416
More the data, better the model, higher will be the accuracy. There are many ways in which the machine learns. It could be either supervised learning, unsupervised learning, or reinforcement learning.

137
00:12:36,576 --> 00:12:48,256
Let's first quickly understand supervised learning. Suppose your friend gives you 1 million coins of three different currencies, say 1 rupee, 1 euro, and 1 dirham. Each coin has different weights.

138
00:12:48,416 --> 00:12:57,696
For example, a coin of 1 rupee weighs 3 grams, 1 euro weighs 7 grams, and 1 dirham weighs 4 grams. Your model will predict the currency of the coin.

139
00:12:57,856 --> 00:13:09,376
Here, your weight becomes the feature of coins, while currency becomes their label. When you feed this data to the machine learning model, it learns which feature is associated with which label.

140
00:13:09,536 --> 00:13:16,096
For example, it will learn that if a coin is of 3 grams, it will be a 1 rupee coin. Let's give a new coin to the machine.

141
00:13:16,256 --> 00:13:24,656
On the basis of the weight of the new coin, your model will predict the currency. Hence, supervised learning uses labeled data to train the model.

142
00:13:24,816 --> 00:13:33,936
Here the machine knew the features of the object and also the labels associated with those features. On this note, let's move to unsupervised learning and see the difference.

143
00:13:34,096 --> 00:13:39,296
Suppose you have cricket dataset of various players with their respective scores and the wickets taken.

144
00:13:39,456 --> 00:13:50,256
When we feed this data set to the machine, the machine identifies the pattern of player performance. So it plots this data with the respective wickets on the x-axis while runs on the y axis.

145
00:13:50,416 --> 00:13:54,016
While looking at the data, you will clearly see that there are two clusters.

146
00:13:54,256 --> 00:14:03,616
The one cluster are the players who scored high runs and took less wickets, while the other cluster is of the players who scored less runs but took many wickets.

147
00:14:03,776 --> 00:14:12,656
So here we interpret these two clusters as batsmen and bowlers. The important point to note here is that there were no labels of batsmen. And bowlers.

148
00:14:12,736 --> 00:14:22,576
Hence, the learning with unlabeled data is unsupervised learning. So we saw supervised learning where the data was labeled and the unsupervised learning where the data was unlabeled.

149
00:14:22,736 --> 00:14:28,736
And then there is reinforcement learning, which is reward-based learning. Or we can say that it works on the principle of feedback.

150
00:14:28,896 --> 00:14:36,256
Here, let's say you provide the system with an image of a dog and ask it to identify it. The system identifies it as a cat.

151
00:14:36,336 --> 00:14:40,096
So you give a negative feedback to the machine saying that it's a dog's image.

152
00:14:40,256 --> 00:14:48,736
The machine will learn from the feedback and finally, if it comes across any other image of a dog, it will be able to classify it correctly. That is reinforcement learning.

153
00:14:48,896 --> 00:14:57,136
To generalize machine learning model, let's see a flowchart. Input is given to a machine learning model, which then gives the output according to the algorithm applied.

154
00:14:57,296 --> 00:15:05,856
If it's right, we take the output as our final result. Else, we provide feedback to the training model and ask it to predict until it learns.

