604
01:01:25,390 --> 01:01:29,348
We can observe in the previous graph that in 2003,

605
01:01:31,214 --> 01:01:34,304
the mean of $3,451

606
01:01:35,982 --> 01:01:38,544
was higher than the median of $1090.

607
01:01:40,814 --> 01:01:44,388
The global income is definitely not evenly distributed.

608
01:01:44,942 --> 01:01:48,868
The majority of people make less than $2,000 each year,

609
01:01:51,278 --> 01:01:56,224
while only a small percentage of the population earns more than $14,000.

610
01:02:03,502 --> 01:02:05,092
Measures of variability.

611
01:02:05,710 --> 01:02:12,004
Dispersion, the measure of central tendencies provide a single value that addresses the full worth.

612
01:02:12,526 --> 01:02:16,964
However, the central tendency cannot depict the viewpoint entirely.

613
01:02:17,550 --> 01:02:22,468
The metric of dispersion helps us focus on the inconsistency in the data spread.

614
01:02:23,278 --> 01:02:26,692
Measures of dispersion describe the spread of the data.

615
01:02:28,814 --> 01:02:35,684
The range, interquartile range, standard deviation, and variance are examples of dispersion measures.

616
01:02:37,390 --> 01:02:37,984
Range.

617
01:02:39,310 --> 01:02:44,612
The range of distribution is the difference between the largest and the smallest amount of data.

618
01:02:46,670 --> 01:02:51,428
The range, for example, does not include all of a series positive aspects.

619
01:02:53,006 --> 01:02:58,116
It concentrates on the most shocking aspects and ignores that aren't considered critical.

620
01:02:58,702 --> 01:03:04,676
For example, for a set 13, 33, 45, 67, 70,

621
01:03:05,870 --> 01:03:07,584
the range is 57.

622
01:03:08,144 --> 01:03:13,412
That is, the maximum of this, which is 70 minus the minimum over here,

623
01:03:14,350 --> 01:03:15,492
which is 13.

624
01:03:16,942 --> 01:03:17,744
Variance.

625
01:03:19,438 --> 01:03:22,784
Variance is the average of all square deviations.

626
01:03:24,238 --> 01:03:31,076
It is defined as the sum of square distance between each point and the mean or the dispersion around the mean.

627
01:03:32,782 --> 01:03:37,348
The standard deviation is used as variance suffers from a unit difference.

628
01:03:39,726 --> 01:03:44,932
Variance can be computed as sigma square summation over x minus mu square

629
01:03:46,094 --> 01:03:47,312
divided by n,

630
01:03:48,398 --> 01:03:50,500
where mu is the mean of the data,

631
01:03:51,406 --> 01:03:53,572
x is the individual data point,

632
01:03:55,022 --> 01:03:57,092
and n is the size of the data.

633
01:03:59,086 --> 01:04:02,084
This representation is for a population data.

634
01:04:03,918 --> 01:04:07,792
For a sample data variance can be computed as x minus

635
01:04:08,718 --> 01:04:10,852
x bar whole square summation

636
01:04:11,790 --> 01:04:14,116
over it divided by n minus one.

637
01:04:15,598 --> 01:04:18,532
Here x bar is the mean of these sample data,

638
01:04:19,502 --> 01:04:21,572
and n is the sample size.

639
01:04:23,022 --> 01:04:26,340
The units of values and variance are not equal,

640
01:04:27,310 --> 01:04:29,892
so another variability measure is used.

641
01:04:31,726 --> 01:04:33,152
Standard deviation.

642
01:04:34,926 --> 01:04:42,404
Standard deviation is a statistical term used to measure the amount of variability or dispersion around a mean.

643
01:04:44,750 --> 01:04:49,028
The standard deviation is calculated as the square root of variance.

644
01:04:49,678 --> 01:04:54,340
It depicts the concentration of the data around the mean of the dataset.

645
01:04:56,846 --> 01:05:02,692
Standard deviation, as indicated previously, can be computed as square root of variance.

646
01:05:03,790 --> 01:05:11,552
For a population data, standard deviation sigma can be computed as square root of summation over xi

647
01:05:11,872 --> 01:05:14,436
minus mu whole square divided by n,

648
01:05:16,782 --> 01:05:18,852
where mu is the mean of the data.

649
01:05:19,380 --> 01:05:22,660
xi are the data points, and n is the size.

650
01:05:23,278 --> 01:05:24,868
Let's consider an example.

651
01:05:26,414 --> 01:05:31,044
Let's find out the mean, variance, and standard deviation for this data.

652
01:05:31,790 --> 01:05:36,484
The data values are 3, 5, 6, 9, and 10.

653
01:05:37,102 --> 01:05:41,700
To find out the mean, we first find the sum of all these data values.

654
01:05:42,990 --> 01:05:46,916
That is 33 and divide it by the count, which is 5.

655
01:05:49,262 --> 01:05:51,472
We get the mean of 6.6.

656
01:05:51,790 --> 01:05:56,100
To compute the variance, we start by computing the deviation.

657
01:05:56,814 --> 01:05:59,172
That is x minus the mean of x.

658
01:06:00,142 --> 01:06:04,868
Here 3 is one of the values of the data, and 6.6 is the mean.

659
01:06:06,862 --> 01:06:10,436
So 3 minus 6.6 square, and we do that

660
01:06:12,110 --> 01:06:15,652
to find out some of all the deviations divided by the count,

661
01:06:17,038 --> 01:06:17,956
which is 5.

662
01:06:19,278 --> 01:06:23,140
We end up getting an overall variance of 6.64.

663
01:06:25,454 --> 01:06:33,444
Standard deviation, as we know, is measured at square root of variance, that is square root of 6.64,

664
01:06:34,062 --> 01:06:36,832
which amounts to 2.576.

665
01:06:38,286 --> 01:06:39,844
Measures of relationship.

666
01:06:41,358 --> 01:06:44,100
Measures of relationship, covariance.

667
01:06:44,686 --> 01:06:48,996
Covariance is the measure of joint variability of two variables.

668
01:06:49,998 --> 01:06:53,796
It measures the direction of the relationship between the variables.

669
01:06:54,542 --> 01:06:58,948
It determines if one variable will cause the other to alter in the same way.

670
01:07:01,070 --> 01:07:03,872
Covariance between variable x and y

671
01:07:04,654 --> 01:07:09,444
can be computed as summation over the product of xi minus x bar

672
01:07:10,766 --> 01:07:14,788
and yi minus y bar the whole divided by n minus one.

673
01:07:17,070 --> 01:07:21,732
Here x bar and y bar are the mean of x and y respectively.

674
01:07:22,350 --> 01:07:27,460
The value of covariance can range from minus infinity to a plus infinity.

675
01:07:29,486 --> 01:07:30,404
Correlation.

676
01:07:31,054 --> 01:07:33,572
Correlation is normalized covariance.

677
01:07:35,758 --> 01:07:39,712
It measures the strength of association between two variables.

678
01:07:39,982 --> 01:07:44,580
The most common measure for correlation is the Pearson correlation coefficient.

679
01:07:45,614 --> 01:07:47,908
Correlation between two variables

680
01:07:49,102 --> 01:07:54,500
x and y can be measured with respect to covariance as covariance between x

681
01:07:55,758 --> 01:08:00,740
and y divided by the standard deviation of x and standard deviation of y.

682
01:08:02,798 --> 01:08:07,108
The value of correlation ranges from a negative one to positive one.

683
01:08:09,102 --> 01:08:10,596
Types of correlation.

684
01:08:12,846 --> 01:08:15,876
Correlation can be either a positive correlation,

685
01:08:16,910 --> 01:08:20,004
zero correlation, or a negative correlation.

686
01:08:23,118 --> 01:08:27,556
The first picture over here represents a perfect positive correlation,

687
01:08:29,294 --> 01:08:32,004
wherein a straight line with a positive slope

688
01:08:33,326 --> 01:08:36,772
is representing the relationship between the two variables.

689
01:08:38,446 --> 01:08:46,148
Zero correlation means that the line representing the relationship between the two variables is horizontal to the x-axis.

690
01:08:48,814 --> 01:08:54,020
Perfect negative correlation can be represented by a straight line with a negative slope.

691
01:08:56,398 --> 01:09:00,516
Correlation equals to one implies a positive relationship.

692
01:09:01,038 --> 01:09:05,668
That is when one variable increases the other variable also increases.

693
01:09:06,190 --> 01:09:10,468
A correlation value of negative one implies a negative relationship.

694
01:09:10,958 --> 01:09:14,596
That is when one variable increases the other decreases.

695
01:09:16,782 --> 01:09:22,916
The correlation coefficient of zero shows that the variables are completely independent of each other.

696
01:09:24,302 --> 01:09:25,860
Let's consider an example.

697
01:09:28,366 --> 01:09:31,524
Here, we have two variables, height and weight.

698
01:09:34,286 --> 01:09:37,124
To compute the correlation between height and weight,

699
01:09:38,510 --> 01:09:42,148
we use the correlation formula as covariance of x

700
01:09:43,374 --> 01:09:48,420
and y divided by standard deviation of x and standard deviation of y.

701
01:09:49,838 --> 01:09:53,764
Here height is the x variable and weight is the y variable.

702
01:09:55,598 --> 01:10:04,292
First to compute covariance, we compute the x minus x bar and y minus y bar values, and then the product of them.

703
01:10:06,286 --> 01:10:09,156
We then compute x minus x bar square

704
01:10:11,150 --> 01:10:17,924
and y minus y bar square values to compute the standard deviations of height and weight respectively.

705
01:10:18,350 --> 01:10:27,908
Correlation, as we know, has been defined as covariance of x and i and y divided by standard deviations of x and y.

706
01:10:29,902 --> 01:10:36,912
This can also be represented as summation over x minus x bar multiplied to y minus y bar

707
01:10:38,094 --> 01:10:42,592
divided by square root of summation over sum of squared deviations.

708
01:10:43,118 --> 01:10:51,108
That is x minus x bar square multiplied to square root of summation over y minus y bar whole square.

709
01:10:51,790 --> 01:10:54,564
That is sum of square deviations for y.

710
01:10:56,622 --> 01:11:00,068
Now, let's find out values to put into this formula.

711
01:11:02,734 --> 01:11:07,236
First, we find out the overall sum of height to get the mean of height,

712
01:11:07,918 --> 01:11:09,712
which is 5.14.

713
01:11:10,190 --> 01:11:14,276
Similarly, we get the sum of weight to get the mean of weight as 50.

714
01:11:14,990 --> 01:11:22,724
We now get the summation over x minus x bar multiplied to y minus y bar to get the numerator for the formula.

715
01:11:23,502 --> 01:11:26,948
Then we compute x minus x bar square summation

716
01:11:28,238 --> 01:11:35,652
and y minus y bar square, that is sum of squared deviation of x and y respectively.

717
01:11:36,238 --> 01:11:43,392
Now we put in the values in this final correlation formula to get a correlation value of 0.889.

718
01:11:45,774 --> 01:11:49,572
This indicates that height and weight have a positive relationship.

719
01:11:50,702 --> 01:11:54,628
It is evident that as height grows, weight also increases.

720
01:11:57,038 --> 01:12:01,392
In this module, we will be talking about expectation and variance.

721
01:12:02,958 --> 01:12:15,072
So the expected value, or we can say mean of a given variable, that we can denote by x is a discrete random variable where it is a weighted average of the possible values that x can take,

722
01:12:15,246 --> 01:12:20,836
and each value is going to be according to the probability of that specific event occurring.

723
01:12:22,094 --> 01:12:31,396
So usually the expected value of x is denoted by a simple formula, where we can define the expectation based on the x parameter,

724
01:12:33,390 --> 01:12:39,716
which is going to be the sum of each possible outcome multiplied by the probability of the outcome occurring.

725
01:12:41,934 --> 01:12:48,900
So, in more concrete terms, the expectation is what we would expect the outcome of an experiment to be on average.

726
01:12:52,206 --> 01:12:54,276
We can take an example for the coin.

727
01:12:55,086 --> 01:12:57,732
If a coin is being tossed 10 times,

728
01:12:58,510 --> 01:13:02,212
then one is most likely to get five heads and five tails.

729
01:13:04,846 --> 01:13:09,828
Same logic can be discussed if we talk about another example of rolling a die.

730
01:13:10,382 --> 01:13:15,940
So there are six possible outcomes when you roll a die, one, two, three, four, five, six.

731
01:13:16,334 --> 01:13:20,388
And each of these has a probability of one by six of occurring.

732
01:13:20,974 --> 01:13:29,168
So we can say that the expectation is going to be one multiplied by the probability of that happening, which is going to be one by six,

733
01:13:29,408 --> 01:13:37,568
plus two by six, plus three by six, plus four by six, plus five by six, plus six by six.

734
01:13:37,774 --> 01:13:41,124
and that is going to give us three.5 as an output.

735
01:13:41,614 --> 01:13:44,452
The expected value is three five.

736
01:13:45,166 --> 01:13:50,948
So if you think about it, 3.5 is halfway between the possible values that I can take.

737
01:13:51,630 --> 01:13:53,700
And this is what we should have expected.

738
01:13:54,510 --> 01:13:57,444
Next, we talk about the concept of variance.

739
01:13:57,998 --> 01:14:05,156
So variance of a random variable allows us to know something about the spread of the possible values of the variable.

740
01:14:05,966 --> 01:14:14,368
So for a discrete random variable x, the variances of x is going to be denoted by using a simple formula that is going to be var

741
01:14:14,608 --> 01:14:22,756
x equals e x minus m, the whole square where m is basically the expected value of the expectation of x.

742
01:14:23,118 --> 01:14:29,380
So this is more like a standard deviation of x, which can also be represented by using this formula.

743
01:14:30,062 --> 01:14:38,308
So the variance does not behave in the same way as expectation when we multiply and add constants to random variables.

744
01:14:40,622 --> 01:14:45,892
So now there are two different type of variants that we can have a fair understanding on.

745
01:14:46,414 --> 01:14:50,768
First of all, we have low variance, and then we have high variance.

746
01:14:52,110 --> 01:15:01,488
So low variance simply means that there is a small variation in the production of the target function, with changes in the trading data set

747
01:15:01,648 --> 01:15:05,168
and at the same time, high variance as we can see here.

748
01:15:05,422 --> 01:15:12,484
High variance shows a large variation in prediction of the target function, with changes in the trading data set.

749
01:15:12,878 --> 01:15:18,404
So a model that shows high variance learns a lot and perform well with the trading data set.

750
01:15:18,798 --> 01:15:22,148
And it does not generalize well with the unseen data set.

751
01:15:22,478 --> 01:15:30,884
And that's why, as a result, such a model gives good results with training data set but shows high error rates on the test data set.

752
01:15:31,438 --> 01:15:38,212
And since the high variance a model learns too much from the data set, it leads to an overfitting of the model.

753
01:15:38,990 --> 01:15:47,812
So model with high variance will be having couple of issues, like it may lead to overfitting, or it may also lead to increase in model complexities.

754
01:15:50,062 --> 01:15:51,812
Next, we have skewness.

755
01:15:53,806 --> 01:15:59,408
So skewness in simple terms is basically a measure of asymmetry of a distribution.

756
01:16:00,110 --> 01:16:05,488
So distribution is asymmetrical when it's left, and right sides are not the mirror images.

757
01:16:06,608 --> 01:16:14,980
Right now, this is a mirrored image, and a distribution can have right positive or we can say negative or it can have zero skewness.

758
01:16:17,742 --> 01:16:23,888
So right skewed in this scenario is basically the distribution is longer on the right side of its peak.

759
01:16:25,454 --> 01:16:30,608
And a left skew distribution is going to be we can say where it is longer on the left side.

760
01:16:32,654 --> 01:16:35,872
So we can see we have this one as a part of right side.

761
01:16:36,174 --> 01:16:38,912
It is more elongated towards the right side.

762
01:16:39,182 --> 01:16:42,564
And this one is more elongated towards the left side.

763
01:16:42,958 --> 01:16:45,636
So we can think of skewness in terms of tails.

764
01:16:46,190 --> 01:16:49,764
A tail is long tampering and the end of a distribution.

765
01:16:50,382 --> 01:16:57,604
So it simply indicates that they are observations at one end of the distribution, but that they are relatively infrequent.

766
01:16:58,126 --> 01:17:03,392
So a right skew distribution has a long tail on the right side, as you can see here.

767
01:17:03,726 --> 01:17:05,668
So the number supports observed.

