Let's start with what is a multi-layer percepitron, or MLP, a lot of time is referred to. And you'll see these abbreviations.

11642
16:29:46,320 --> 16:29:52,960
I'll be honest, I have to write them down on a piece of paper and go through them because I never remember what they all mean, even though I play with them all the time.

11643
16:29:53,120 --> 16:30:01,600
What is a multilayer percipitron? Well, if you look at the image on the right, it's very similar to what we just looked at. You have your input layer, your hidden layer, and your output layer.

11644
16:30:01,680 --> 16:30:08,720
And that's exactly what this is. It has the same structure of a single layer perceptron with one or more hidden layers, except the input layer.

11645
16:30:08,880 --> 16:30:12,720
Each node in the other layers uses a nonlinear activation function.

11646
16:30:12,880 --> 16:30:22,320
What that means is your input layer is your data coming in, and then your activation function is based upon all those nodes and weights being added together, and then it has the output.

11647
16:30:22,560 --> 16:30:28,000
MLP uses supervised learning method called backpropagation for training the model.

11648
16:30:28,320 --> 16:30:39,840
Very key word there is backpropagation. Single layer perceptron can classify only linear separable classes with binary output, zero, one, but the MLP can classify nonlinear classes.

11649
16:30:40,080 --> 16:30:45,760
So let's break this down just a little bit the multi-layer percipitron with an input layer and a hidden layer and an output layer.

11650
16:30:45,920 --> 16:30:53,280
As you see that it comes in there, it adds up all the numbers and weights, depending on how your setup is, that then goes to the next layer,

11651
16:30:53,440 --> 16:30:58,160
that then goes to the next hidden layer if you have multiple hidden layers, and finally to the output layer.

11652
16:30:58,560 --> 16:31:08,160
The back propagation takes the error that it sees. So whatever the output is, it says, hey, this has an error to it, it's wrong. And it sends that error backwards from where it came from.

11653
16:31:08,270 --> 16:31:15,920
And there's a lot of different functions used to train this based on that error and how that error goes backwards in the nodes.

11654
16:31:16,320 --> 16:31:19,520
So forward is you get your answers, backward is for training.

11655
16:31:19,680 --> 16:31:28,320
You see this every day. Even my Google Pixel phone has this. They train the neural network, which takes a lot more data to train than it does to use.

