16:35:14,958 --> 16:35:20,208
And you can look at the bias if you remember from your Euclidean geometry, you draw a straight line.

11690
16:35:20,430 --> 16:35:23,808
The formula for that line has a y coordinate at the end.

11691
16:35:23,968 --> 16:35:31,008
It's always cx plus m or something like that, where m is where it crosses the y coordinates if you're doing a straight line.

11692
16:35:31,168 --> 16:35:36,368
With these weights, it's very similar, but a lot of times we just add it in as its own weight.

11693
16:35:36,528 --> 16:35:45,088
We take it as a node of a one value coming in, and then we compute its new weight, and that's how we compute that bias, just like we compute all the other weights coming in.

11694
16:35:45,248 --> 16:35:54,688
The node which gets fired depends on the y value, and then we have a step function. And the step function, this is where, remember I said it's going to get complicated and simple all at the same time.

11695
16:35:54,928 --> 16:36:04,768
We have a lot of different step functions. We have the sigmoid function, we have just a standard step function, we have the ReLU, it's pronounced like Ray, the ray from the sun

11696
16:36:04,928 --> 16:36:15,488
and lu, like a name. So ReLU function, and we have the tangent h function. And if you look at these, they all have something similar. They all either force it to be one value or the other.

11697
16:36:15,694 --> 16:36:25,248
They force it to be in the case of the first three a zero or one, and in the last one, it's either a minus one or a one. And you can easily convert that to a zero, one, yes, no, true, false.

11698
16:36:25,488 --> 16:36:33,648
And on this, one of the most common ones is the step function itself, because there is no middle value, there is no discrepancy that says,

11699
16:36:33,808 --> 16:36:42,928
well, I'm not quite sure. But as you get into different models, probably the most commonly used used to be the sigmoid was most commonly used, but I see the ReLU used more often.

11700
16:36:43,168 --> 16:36:50,048
Really, depending on what you're doing, you just have to play with these and find out which one works best, depending on the data and your output.

11701
16:36:50,208 --> 16:36:59,168
The reason to have a non-zero one answer or something kind of in the middle is when you're looking at this and it's coming out, you can actually process that

11702
16:36:59,328 --> 16:37:08,848
middle ground as part of the answer into another neural network. So it might be that the ReLU function says, hey, this is only a 0.6, not a 1.

11703
16:37:09,088 --> 16:37:17,088
And even though the one is what's going into the next neural network or the next hidden layer as an input, the 0.6

11704
16:37:17,328 --> 16:37:25,968
value might also be going in there to let you know, hey, this is not a straight up one or a straight up zero, it's someplace in the middle. This is a little uncertain what's coming out here.

11705
16:37:26,128 --> 16:37:31,648
So it's a very powerful tool. But in the basic neural network, you usually just use the step function. It's yes or no.

11706
16:37:31,888 --> 16:37:40,928
Let's take a big step back and take a uh kind of an overview. The next function is what is a cost function that we're going to cover.

11707
16:37:41,168 --> 16:37:48,848
This is so important because this is your end result that you're going to do over and over again and use to decide whether the model is working or not,

11708
16:37:49,008 --> 16:37:55,008
whether you need to try a different step function, whether you need to try a different activation, whether you need to try a fully different model used.

11709
16:37:55,408 --> 16:37:56,768
so what is the cost function?

11710
16:37:57,008 --> 16:38:01,488
Cost function is the measure to evaluate how good your model's performance is.

11711
16:38:01,744 --> 16:38:12,304
It is also referred to as loss or error used to compute the error of the output layer during backpropagation. There's our backpropagation where we're training our model. That's one of our key words.

11712
16:38:12,544 --> 16:38:23,264
Mean squared error is an example of a popular cost function. And so here we have the cost function c equals half of y minus y predicted. and then you square that.

11713
16:38:23,424 --> 16:38:32,704
So the first thing is you know, real quick, if you haven't done statistics, this is not a percentage. It's not a percentage of how accurate it is, it's just a measurement of the error.

11714
16:38:32,864 --> 16:38:42,064
And we take that error for training it, and we push that error backwards through the neural network, and we use that through the different training functions, depending on what model you're using,

11715
16:38:42,304 --> 16:38:51,024
to train the neural network. So when you deploy the network, you're usually done training it, because it takes a lot of computational force to train it. Um this is a very simple model.

11716
16:38:51,184 --> 16:38:56,704
And so you deploy the trained one, but we want to know how your error is. And so how do we do that? Well, you split your data.

11717
16:38:56,864 --> 16:39:06,384
Part of your data is for training and part of your data is for testing, and then we can also test the error on there. So that's very important. And then we're going to go one more step on this.

11718
16:39:06,544 --> 16:39:15,584
We gotta look at both the local and the global setup. It might work great to test your data on what you have on your computer, but that's different than in the field.

11719
16:39:15,744 --> 16:39:24,544
So when we're talking about all these different tests in the error test as far as your loss, you don't you want to make sure that you're in a closed environment when you do initial testing,

11720
16:39:24,624 --> 16:39:31,104
but you also want to open that up and make sure you follow up with the testing on the larger scale of data because it will change. It might not fit the larger scale.

11721
16:39:31,184 --> 16:39:37,904
There might be something in there in the way you brought the data in specifically, or the data group you used, or any of those could cause an error.

11722
16:39:38,062 --> 16:39:43,344
So it's very important to remember that we're looking at both the local and the global context of our error.

11723
16:39:43,504 --> 16:39:52,384
And just one other side note on a lot of the newer models of neural networks, by comparing the error we get on the data, our training data,

11724
16:39:52,624 --> 16:40:01,264
with a portion of the test data, we can actually figure out how good the model is, whether it's overfitted or not. We'll go into that a little bit more as we go into some of the different models.

11725
16:40:01,504 --> 16:40:11,744
So we have our output, we're able to figure out the error on it based on the square means usually, although there's other functions used. So we want to talk about what is gradient descent.

11726
16:40:11,904 --> 16:40:19,664
Another vocabulary word. Gradient descent is an optimization algorithm to minimize the cost function or to minimize the error.

11727
16:40:19,918 --> 16:40:23,024
Aim is to find the local or global minima of a function.

11728
16:40:23,184 --> 16:40:32,704
Determine the direction the model should take to reduce the error. So as we're looking at this, we have our squared error that we just figured out, based on the cost function.

11729
16:40:32,784 --> 16:40:38,384
It says how bad is my model fitting the data I just put through it. And then we want to reduce that error.

11730
16:40:38,544 --> 16:40:46,384
So how do you figure out what direction to do that in? Well, it could be that you're looking at just that line of that line of data coming in, so that would be a local minima.

11731
16:40:46,464 --> 16:40:55,984
We want to know the error of that particular setup coming in. And then you have your global, your global minima. We want to minimize it based on the overall data we're putting through it.

11732
16:40:56,144 --> 16:41:05,744
And with this, we can figure out the global minimum cost. We want to take all those local minimum costs of each piece of data coming in and figure out the global one.

11733
16:41:05,904 --> 16:41:08,224
How are we going to adjust this model to fit all the data?

11734
16:41:08,494 --> 16:41:16,704
We don't want it to be biased just on three or four lines of data coming in. We want it to kind of extrapolate a general answer for all the data coming in.

11735
16:41:17,024 --> 16:41:27,344
This, of course, we mentioned it briefly about backpropagation. This is where it really comes in handy is training our model. Neural network technique to minimize the cost function.

11736
16:41:27,584 --> 16:41:33,664
helps to improve the performance of the network, backpropagates the error, and updates the weights to reduce the error.

11737
16:41:33,934 --> 16:41:43,488
So as you can see here is a very nice depiction of a backpropagation. We have our predicted Y coming out, and then we have, since it's a training set, we already know the answer.

11738
16:41:43,648 --> 16:41:52,048
And the answer comes back and based on case of the square means was one of the functions we looked at. One of the activation functions based on cost function.

