15002
22:11:12,718 --> 22:11:17,312
So clustering, especially k-means clustering, uses distance measure.

15003
22:11:17,422 --> 22:11:20,192
So let's take a look at what is distance measure.

15004
22:11:20,334 --> 22:11:27,044
So while these are the different types of clustering, in this video we will focus on k-means clustering.

15005
22:11:27,406 --> 22:11:32,832
So distance measure tells how similar some objects are.

15006
22:11:33,102 --> 22:11:37,988
So the similarity is measured using what is known as distance measure.

15007
22:11:38,272 --> 22:11:41,220
And what are the various types of distance measures?

15008
22:11:41,486 --> 22:11:50,404
There is Euclidean distance, there is Manhattan distance, then we have squared Euclidean distance measure and cosine distance measure.

15009
22:11:50,592 --> 22:11:54,660
These are some of the distance measures supported by k-means clustering.

15010
22:11:54,832 --> 22:11:56,672
Let's take a look at each of these.

15011
22:11:57,038 --> 22:12:01,632
What is Euclidean distance measure? This is nothing but the distance between two points.

15012
22:12:01,806 --> 22:12:06,032
So we have learnt in high school how to find the distance between two points.

15013
22:12:06,272 --> 22:12:15,552
This is a little sophisticated formula for that, but we know a simpler one is square root of y2 minus y1 whole square plus x2

15014
22:12:15,712 --> 22:12:17,284
minus x1 whole square.

15015
22:12:17,392 --> 22:12:23,632
So this is an extension of that formula. So that is the Euclidean distance between two points.

15016
22:12:23,982 --> 22:12:26,980
What is the squared Euclidean distance measure?

15017
22:12:27,182 --> 22:12:32,836
It's nothing but the square of the Euclidean distance as the name suggests.

15018
22:12:33,198 --> 22:12:38,148
So instead of taking the square root, we leave the square as it is.

15019
22:12:38,446 --> 22:12:49,060
And then we have Manhattan distance measure. In case of Manhattan distance, it is the sum of the distances across the x-axis and the y-axis.

15020
22:12:49,326 --> 22:12:54,192
And note that we are taking the absolute value so that the negative values don't come into play.

15021
22:12:54,318 --> 22:12:58,404
So that is the Manhattan distance measure. Then we have cosine distance measure.

15022
22:12:58,592 --> 22:13:05,152
In this case, we take the angle between the two vectors formed by joining the points from the origin.

15023
22:13:05,486 --> 22:13:08,032
So that is the cosine distance measure.

15024
22:13:08,272 --> 22:13:14,884
Okay, so that was a quick overview about the various distance measures that are supported by k-means.

15025
22:13:15,152 --> 22:13:19,364
Now let's go and check how exactly k-means clustering works.

15026
22:13:19,886 --> 22:13:26,692
Okay, so this is how k-means clustering works. This is like a flowchart of the whole process.

15027
22:13:27,072 --> 22:13:35,472
There is a starting point, and then we specify the number of clusters that we want. Now there are a couple of ways of doing this.

15028
22:13:35,792 --> 22:13:38,272
We can do by trial and error.

15029
22:13:38,352 --> 22:13:48,352
So we specify a certain number. maybe k is equal to 3 or 4 or 5 to start with. and then as we progress, we keep changing until we get the best clusters.

15030
22:13:48,592 --> 22:13:54,752
or there is a technique called elbow technique whereby we can determine the value of k.

15031
22:13:54,926 --> 22:13:56,672
what should be the best value of k?

15032
22:13:56,912 --> 22:13:58,512
how many clusters should be formed?

15033
22:13:58,766 --> 22:14:06,512
So once we have the value of k, we specify that, and then the system will assign that many centroids.

15034
22:14:06,592 --> 22:14:14,980
So it picks randomly that to start with randomly that many points that are considered to be the centroids of these clusters.

15035
22:14:15,232 --> 22:14:28,032
And then it measures the distance of each of the data points from these centroids and assigns those points to the corresponding centroid from which the distance is minimum.

15036
22:14:28,192 --> 22:14:33,792
So each data point will be assigned to the centroid which is closest to it.

15037
22:14:33,966 --> 22:14:38,832
And thereby we have k number of initial clusters.

15038
22:14:39,086 --> 22:14:41,632
However, this is not the final clusters.

15039
22:14:41,806 --> 22:14:43,712
The next step it does is

15040
22:14:44,560 --> 22:14:55,108
For the new groups, for the clusters that have been formed, it calculates the main position, thereby calculates the new centroid position.

15041
22:14:55,470 --> 22:14:59,520
The position of the centroid moves compared to the randomly allocated one.

15042
22:14:59,662 --> 22:15:06,340
So it's an iterative process. Once again, the distance of each point is measured from this new centroid point.

15043
22:15:06,640 --> 22:15:16,320
And if required, the data points are reallocated to the new centroids and the mean position or the new centroid is calculated once again.

15044
22:15:16,494 --> 22:15:24,160
If the centroid moves, then the iteration continues, which means the convergence has not happened, the clustering has not converged.

15045
22:15:24,240 --> 22:15:29,988
So as long as there is a movement of the centroid, this iteration keeps happening.

15046
22:15:30,240 --> 22:15:38,628
But once the centroid stops moving, which means that the cluster has converged or the clustering process has converged,

15047
22:15:38,880 --> 22:15:40,560
that will be the end result.

15048
22:15:40,974 --> 22:15:49,760
So now we have the final position of the centroid and the data points are allocated accordingly to the closest centroid.

15049
22:15:49,934 --> 22:15:59,520
I know it's a little difficult to understand from this simple flowchart, so let's do a little bit of visualization and see if we can explain it better.

15050
22:15:59,790 --> 22:16:00,720
Let's take an example.

15051
22:16:00,960 --> 22:16:13,760
If we have a data set for a grocery shop, so let's say we have a data set for a grocery shop and now we want to find out how many clusters this has to be spread across.

15052
22:16:14,062 --> 22:16:17,600
So how do we find the optimum number of clusters?

15053
22:16:17,838 --> 22:16:20,036
There is a technique called the elbow method.

15054
22:16:20,240 --> 22:16:26,320
So when these clusters are formed, there is a parameter called within sum of squares.

15055
22:16:26,720 --> 22:16:35,440
And the lower this value is, the better the cluster is. That means all these points are very close to each other.

15056
22:16:35,680 --> 22:16:46,960
So we use this within sum of squares as a measure to find the optimum number of clusters that can be formed for a given data set.

