So let's start with beginner level questions and number one is what is machine learning?

15384
22:58:31,472 --> 22:58:43,684
So machine learning is a subset of artificial intelligence that involves the use of algorithms and statistical models to enable computers to perform tasks without explicit instructions.

15385
22:58:44,046 --> 22:58:46,596
That is, by relying on patterns and interference.

15386
22:58:47,182 --> 22:58:51,684
And now moving to number second question, that is what are the different types of machine learning?

15387
22:58:52,238 --> 22:59:01,220
So the three main types of machine learning are number one is supervised learning, and then comes unsupervised learning, and then there is reinforcement learning.

15388
22:59:01,902 --> 22:59:03,872
Now moving to the next question that is third.

15389
22:59:04,432 --> 22:59:06,244
That is what is supervised learning?

15390
22:59:06,798 --> 22:59:14,436
So supervised learning involves training a model on a label data set, which means each training example is paired with an output label.

15391
22:59:14,926 --> 22:59:17,956
The model learns to predict the output from the input data.

15392
22:59:18,734 --> 22:59:22,592
Now moving to the fourth question, that is what is unsupervised learning.

15393
22:59:22,958 --> 22:59:27,872
So unsupervised involves training a model on data that does not have labeled responses.

15394
22:59:28,112 --> 22:59:31,872
The model tries to learn the patterns and the structure from the input data.

15395
22:59:32,174 --> 22:59:34,340
So guys, these are the beginner level questions.

15396
22:59:34,574 --> 22:59:38,340
And now we'll move to the fifth question. That is what is reinforcement learning.

15397
22:59:38,734 --> 22:59:47,524
So reinforcement learning is a type of machine learning where an agent learns to make decisions by performing actions and receiving rewards or penalties.

15398
22:59:47,886 --> 22:59:50,432
The goal is to maximize the accumulative reward.

15399
22:59:50,926 --> 22:59:55,012
So now moving to the sixth question, that is what is a model in machine learning.

15400
22:59:55,534 --> 23:00:00,432
So a model in machine learning is a mathematical representation of a real-world process.

15401
23:00:00,942 --> 23:00:07,152
It is trained on data to recognize patterns and make predictions or decisions based on new data.

15402
23:00:07,438 --> 23:00:10,192
So now moving to the seventh question, that is what is overfitting.

15403
23:00:10,352 --> 23:00:17,252
So overfitting occurs when a machine learning model performs well on the training data, but poorly on new unseen data.

15404
23:00:17,552 --> 23:00:23,780
It indicates that the model has learned the noise and details in the training data instead of the actual patterns.

15405
23:00:24,206 --> 23:00:27,632
So now coming to question number eight, that is what is underfitting.

15406
23:00:28,110 --> 23:00:33,792
So underfitting occurs when a machine learning model is too simple to capture the underlying patterns in the data.

15407
23:00:34,032 --> 23:00:37,472
It performs poorly on both the training data and new data.

15408
23:00:38,286 --> 23:00:43,104
Now moving to the next question, that is ninth question, and the question is what is a confusion matrix?

15409
23:00:43,438 --> 23:00:48,644
So confusion matrix is a table used to evaluate the performance of a classification model.

15410
23:00:49,134 --> 23:00:56,144
It summarizes the number of correct and incorrect predictions made by the model and that is categorized by each class.

15411
23:00:56,398 --> 23:00:59,524
Now moving to the tenth question, that is what is cross-validation.

15412
23:00:59,982 --> 23:01:07,908
So cross-validation is a technique for assessing how the results of a statistical analysis will generalize to an independent dataset.

15413
23:01:08,366 --> 23:01:16,304
It involves partitioning the data into subsets, training the model on some subsets, and validating it on the remaining subsets.

15414
23:01:16,526 --> 23:01:23,140
So this was all about that is the 10th question or the overall 1 to 10 questions for beginner level.

15415
23:01:23,566 --> 23:01:27,620
Now we'll move to intermediate level and here we'll cover 10 questions.

15416
23:01:28,078 --> 23:01:32,004
So we'll start with 11th question that is what is a ROC curve.

15417
23:01:32,366 --> 23:01:36,580
So ROC that is receiver operating characteristic curve.

15418
23:01:37,102 --> 23:01:42,244
It is a graphical representation of a classifier's performance across different thresholds.

15419
23:01:42,606 --> 23:01:48,388
It plots the true positive rate that is TPR against a false positive rate that is FPR.

15420
23:01:48,974 --> 23:01:52,612
Now moving to the 12th question, that is what is precision and recall.

15421
23:01:52,864 --> 23:01:58,788
So precision is the ratio of correctly predicted positive observations to the total predicted positives.

15422
23:01:59,182 --> 23:02:05,028
And recall is the ratio of correctly predicted positive observations to all actual positives.

15423
23:02:05,486 --> 23:02:15,108
So the formula is precision equal to TP slash TP plus FP and the recall is TP slash TP plus Fn.

15424
23:02:15,918 --> 23:02:20,292
So now we'll move to the 13th question that is what is the F1 score?

15425
23:02:20,878 --> 23:02:25,028
So the F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall.

15426
23:02:25,198 --> 23:02:31,524
It provides a balance between the two metrics and is useful when you need to balance precision and recall.

15427
23:02:32,014 --> 23:02:38,948
F1 score is equal to twice into precision into recall and that is divided by precision plus recall.

15428
23:02:39,726 --> 23:02:43,940
Now we'll move to the 14th question and here we will cover regularization.

15429
23:02:44,494 --> 23:02:47,076
So the question is what is regularization?

15430
23:02:47,264 --> 23:02:53,188
So it is a technique used to prevent overfitting by adding a penalty to the model's complexity.

15431
23:02:53,582 --> 23:02:59,588
And the common types of regularization include L1, that is lasso, and L2, ridge regularization.

15432
23:03:00,174 --> 23:03:04,644
Now we'll move to the 15th question that is what is the bias variance trade-off.

15433
23:03:05,134 --> 23:03:13,668
So the bias variance tradeoff is a fundamental issue in machine learning that involves balancing the error introduced by the model's assumptions

15434
23:03:14,030 --> 23:03:16,224
and the error due to model complexity.

15435
23:03:16,398 --> 23:03:19,588
So a good model should have low bias and low variance.

15436
23:03:20,110 --> 23:03:24,004
Now we'll move to the question number 16, that is what is feature engineering.

15437
23:03:24,398 --> 23:03:32,464
So feature engineering is the process of creating new features or modifying existing ones to improve the performance of a machine learning model.

15438
23:03:32,654 --> 23:03:39,428
It involves techniques like normalization and coding categorical variables and creating interaction terms.

15439
23:03:40,046 --> 23:03:44,612
So now we'll move to question number 17 and that is about gradient descent.

15440
23:03:44,878 --> 23:03:46,980
So the question is what is gradient descent?

15441
23:03:47,184 --> 23:03:54,692
And your answer is gradient descent is an optimization algorithm used to minimize the cost function in machine learning models

15442
23:03:54,944 --> 23:04:01,956
and it iteratively adjusts the model parameters in the direction of the steepest descent of the cost function.

15443
23:04:02,670 --> 23:04:08,964
So with this we'll move to the 18th question and that we'll cover with the difference between bagging and boosting.

15444
23:04:09,678 --> 23:04:12,708
So the question is what is the difference between bagging and boosting?

15445
23:04:12,816 --> 23:04:17,988
And you could answer this with starting with bagging, that is bootstrap aggregating.

15446
23:04:18,414 --> 23:04:24,096
That involves training multiple models on different subsets of the data and averaging their predictions.

15447
23:04:24,302 --> 23:04:32,036
Then comes boosting that involves training models sequentially with each new model focusing on correcting the errors of the previous ones.

15448
23:04:32,494 --> 23:04:36,548
And then we have the question number 19, that is what is a decision tree.

15449
23:04:36,814 --> 23:04:42,916
So a decision tree is a known parametric supervised learning algorithm used for classification and regression.

15450
23:04:43,246 --> 23:04:49,732
It splits the data into subsets based on the value of input features, resulting in a tree-like structure of decisions.

15451
23:04:50,158 --> 23:04:53,380
Now we'll move to question number 20, that is what is a random forest.

15452
23:04:53,774 --> 23:05:02,336
So random forest is an ensemble learning method that combines multiple decision trees to improve the accuracy and robustness of the model.

15453
23:05:02,576 --> 23:05:09,188
It builds each tree using a random subset of features and data points and then averages their predictions.

15454
23:05:09,614 --> 23:05:12,932
So these were the questions that are for the intermediate level.

15455
23:05:13,056 --> 23:05:19,844
And these are just the basic questions, or I will just say the theoretical questions that can be asked in an interview.

15456
23:05:20,174 --> 23:05:26,016
So be prepared for that. Now we'll move to the advanced level interview questions and we'll start with question number 21.

15457
23:05:26,096 --> 23:05:28,656
And here also we'll cover the 10 questions.

15458
23:05:28,942 --> 23:05:35,296
So number one question, or that is 21 question, and the question is what is a support vector machine?

15459
23:05:35,534 --> 23:05:40,996
So support vector machine is a supervised learning algorithm used for classification and regression.

15460
23:05:41,390 --> 23:05:47,492
It finds the optimal hyperplane that maximizes the margin between different classes in the feature space.

15461
23:05:47,886 --> 23:05:52,164
And then comes question number 22, that is what is principal component analysis.

15462
23:05:52,526 --> 23:06:02,340
So principal component analysis is a dimensionality reduction technique that transforms high-dimensional data into a lower dimensional space by finding the directions,

15463
23:06:02,766 --> 23:06:06,660
that is, principal components that maximize the variance in the data.

15464
23:06:07,086 --> 23:06:10,496
And then comes the question number 23, that is what is a neural network.

15465
23:06:10,606 --> 23:06:20,996
So a neural network is a series of algorithms that attempt to recognize underlying relationships in a set of data through a process that mimics the way the human brain operates.

15466
23:06:21,262 --> 23:06:24,772
It consists of a layer of interconnected nodes or neurons.

15467
23:06:25,230 --> 23:06:28,580
And then comes the question number 24, that is what is deep learning.

15468
23:06:29,056 --> 23:06:34,496
So deep learning is a subset of machine learning that involves neural networks with many layers, that is,

15469
23:06:34,702 --> 23:06:40,196
deep neural networks, and it is particularly effective for tasks like image and speech recognition.

15470
23:06:40,654 --> 23:06:46,948
Now I'll move to question number 25, that is, what is convolutional neural network, that is CNN.

15471
23:06:47,376 --> 23:06:58,244
So we will start the answer by answering the interviewer that a convolutional neural network is a type of deep running model specifically designed for processing structured grid data like images.

15472
23:06:58,670 --> 23:07:05,412
It uses convolutional layers to extract special features or the spatial features and patterns from the input data.

15473
23:07:05,934 --> 23:07:07,748
Now we'll move to the question number 26.

15474
23:07:08,110 --> 23:07:11,396
That is, what is a recurrent neural network or RNN?

15475
23:07:11,918 --> 23:07:20,228
So a recurrent neural network is a type of neural network designed for sequential data and it has connections that form directed cycles,

15476
23:07:20,416 --> 23:07:25,696
allowing it to maintain a memory of previous inputs and process sequences of data.

15477
23:07:25,998 --> 23:07:34,276
So this is all about question number 26, and now we will cover the question number 27 that is what is the difference between batch gradient descent and

15478
23:07:34,576 --> 23:07:36,420
stochastic gradient descent.

15479
23:07:37,070 --> 23:07:46,016
So batch gradient descent computes the gradient of the cost function using the entire training dataset, while stochastic gradient descent that is

15480
23:07:46,256 --> 23:07:50,660
SGD computes the gradient using only one training example at a time.

15481
23:07:51,086 --> 23:07:53,732
So SGD is faster but noisier.

15482
23:07:54,318 --> 23:07:56,080
Now we'll move to question number 28.

15483
23:07:56,174 --> 23:07:58,400
That is what is dropout in neural networks.

15484
23:07:58,638 --> 23:08:09,188
So dropout is a regularization technique used in neural networks to prevent overfitting, and it involves randomly setting a fraction of the neurons to zero during training,

15485
23:08:09,600 --> 23:08:12,804
forcing the network to learn more robust features.

15486
23:08:13,326 --> 23:08:17,540
And now we'll move to question number 29 and that will be about transfer learning.

15487
23:08:17,742 --> 23:08:19,940
And your question is, what is transfer learning?

15488
23:08:20,398 --> 23:08:29,040
So we'll answer this to the interviewer by starting that transfer learning is a technique in machine learning where a model developed for one task is reused

15489
23:08:29,200 --> 23:08:33,120
as the starting point for a model on a second related task.

15490
23:08:33,326 --> 23:08:37,668
It is particularly useful when there is limited data available for the second task.

15491
23:08:38,254 --> 23:08:41,444
Now we'll move to the last question and the 30th question.

15492
23:08:41,680 --> 23:08:46,240
So that is what is a generative adversarial network that is GN.

15493
23:08:46,830 --> 23:08:48,320
So you can start answering this.

15494
23:08:48,494 --> 23:08:58,692
So generative adversarial network is a type of deep learning model consisting of two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator that are trained simultaneously.

15495
23:08:59,054 --> 23:09:08,836
The generator creates fake data while the discriminator tries to distinguish between real and fake data, leading to the generator producing increasingly realistic data.

15496
23:09:09,040 --> 23:09:18,240
And these questions and answers are over, and these covers a wide range of topics in machine learning and should help prepare for interviews at various levels.

15497
23:09:18,542 --> 23:09:22,500
Today we will learn about some of the machine learning and deep learning interview questions.

15498
23:09:23,854 --> 23:09:25,636
So let's begin with the first question.

15499
23:09:27,118 --> 23:09:30,628
The first question is how to detect outliers in data.

15500
23:09:31,630 --> 23:09:33,764
So in data analytics and machine learning,

15501
23:09:34,542 --> 23:09:40,516
you often find data points that lie at an abnormal distance from other points in a random sample from a population.

15502
23:09:41,040 --> 23:09:42,720
Those are called outliers.

15503
23:09:42,926 --> 23:09:46,724
Now outliers in data can significantly impact any prediction analysis.

15504
23:09:47,120 --> 23:09:50,212
There are majorly three different methods to treat outliers.

15505
23:09:50,800 --> 23:09:53,092
First, we have the univariate method.

15506
23:09:53,646 --> 23:09:56,388
It is one of the simplest methods for detecting outliers.

15507
23:09:56,640 --> 23:09:59,172
The univariate method uses box plots.

15508
23:09:59,440 --> 23:10:03,492
A box plot is a graphical display for describing the distributions of the data.

15509
23:10:04,160 --> 23:10:08,100
Box plots use the median and the lower and upper quartiles.

15510
23:10:09,294 --> 23:10:12,804
This method looks for data points with extreme values on one variable.

15511
23:10:13,358 --> 23:10:15,428
Next, we have the multivariate method.

15512
23:10:15,760 --> 23:10:20,516
So the multivariate outliers can be found in an n-dimensional space having n features.

15513
23:10:20,720 --> 23:10:24,228
We look for unusual combinations of all the variables in this method.

15514
23:10:24,560 --> 23:10:27,012
Finally, we have Minkowski error.

15515
23:10:27,758 --> 23:10:31,556
This method reduces the contribution of potential outliers in the training process.

15516
23:10:31,760 --> 23:10:37,476
The Minkowski error is a loss index that is more insensitive to outliers than the standard mean squared error.

15517
23:10:37,934 --> 23:10:39,684
Now moving on to the second question:

15518
23:10:40,398 --> 23:10:42,148
What is a confusion matrix?

15519
23:10:43,022 --> 23:10:51,556
So a confusion matrix is a table that is used to describe the performance of a classification model and a set of test data for which the true values are already known.

15520
23:10:52,080 --> 23:10:55,332
The target variable has two values, positive or negative.

15521
23:10:55,840 --> 23:11:00,676
The columns represent the actual values of the target variable, which you can see here.

15522
23:11:01,040 --> 23:11:05,860
The rows represent the predicted values of the target variable, which you can see here.

15523
23:11:06,254 --> 23:11:10,084
Now there are four important terms that are related to confusion matrix.

15524
23:11:10,480 --> 23:11:13,860
First, we have true positive, which is this one.

15525
23:11:14,926 --> 23:11:18,180
So in true positive, the predicted value matches the actual value.

15526
23:11:18,702 --> 23:11:22,692
So the actual value was positive and the model also predicted a positive value.

15527
23:11:23,280 --> 23:11:25,220
Then we have true negative,

15528
23:11:25,998 --> 23:11:28,100
which is also represented as Tn.

15529
23:11:28,430 --> 23:11:32,164
The true negative depicts the predicted value matches the actual value.

15530
23:11:32,640 --> 23:11:36,420
Now the actual value was negative and the model predicted a negative value.

15531
23:11:37,230 --> 23:11:39,360
Next we have false positive.

15532
23:11:39,726 --> 23:11:42,880
Now false positive is also known as type 1 error.

15533
23:11:43,438 --> 23:11:46,660
In false positive, the predicted value was falsely predicted.

15534
23:11:47,054 --> 23:11:50,948
The actual value was negative, but the model predicted a positive value.

15535
23:11:51,360 --> 23:11:56,260
Finally, we have false negative, now false negative is also known as type 2 error.

15536
23:11:57,198 --> 23:12:00,548
So, in false negative, the predicted value was falsely predicted.

15537
23:12:00,880 --> 23:12:04,580
The actual value was positive, but the model predicted a negative value.

15538
23:12:04,880 --> 23:12:06,660
Now moving to a third question,

15539
23:12:07,310 --> 23:12:09,636
which is explain the ROC curve.

15540
23:12:10,446 --> 23:12:16,356
Now the ROC curve is one of the most important evaluation matrix for checking the performance of any classification model.

15541
23:12:17,040 --> 23:12:20,420
ROC stands for receiver operating characteristic.

15542
23:12:20,720 --> 23:12:25,764
Receiver operating characteristic or ROC curve is a method to compare the diagnostic tests.

15543
23:12:26,160 --> 23:12:32,800
The ROC curve is created by plotting the true positive rate against the false positive rate at various threshold settings.

15544
23:12:33,006 --> 23:12:38,404
So here on the y-axis you have the true positive rate, on the x-axis we have the false positive rate.

15545
23:12:38,830 --> 23:12:45,828
The true positive rate indicates the proportion of observations that were correctly predicted to be positive out of all positive observations.

15546
23:12:46,190 --> 23:12:54,500
Similarly, the false positive rate is the proportion of observations that are incorrectly predicted to be positive out of all negative observations.

15547
23:12:55,662 --> 23:13:05,360
You can take an example, suppose in medical testing, the true positive rate is the rate in which people are correctly identified to test positive for the disease in question,

15548
23:13:05,550 --> 23:13:07,428
let's say the coronavirus testing.

15549
23:13:07,854 --> 23:13:10,880
ROC does not depend on any class distribution.

15550
23:13:11,120 --> 23:13:16,160
This makes it useful for evaluating classifiers predicting rare events such as diseases or disasters.

15551
23:13:16,462 --> 23:13:18,532
Now moving to the fourth question, we have

15552
23:13:19,182 --> 23:13:21,636
what are the assumptions for linear regression?

15553
23:13:22,382 --> 23:13:30,532
So linear regression analysis is used for modeling the relationship between a single dependent variable y and one or more feature or predictor variables.

15554
23:13:31,278 --> 23:13:35,268
Some of the important assumptions for linear regression are so first

15555
23:13:35,918 --> 23:13:37,220
they should have linearity.

15556
23:13:37,678 --> 23:13:42,596
So linear regression needs the relationship between the independent and the dependent variables to be linear.

15557
23:13:43,200 --> 23:13:48,612
It is also crucial to check for outliers since linear regression is sensitive to outlier effects.

15558
23:13:48,942 --> 23:13:51,108
Next, we have homo schedasticity.

15559
23:13:51,630 --> 23:13:58,160
Homo schedasticity illustrates a situation in which the error term, that is, the noise or random disturbance

15560
23:13:58,240 --> 23:14:03,716
in the relationship between the features and the target variable is the same across all levels of the dependent variables.

15561
23:14:04,270 --> 23:14:08,484
Third, we have independence, so observations should be independent of each other.

15562
23:14:09,280 --> 23:14:15,236
Finally, we have no multi-colinearity, so there should be little or no multi-colinearity.

15563
23:14:15,630 --> 23:14:18,340
Independent variables should not be too highly correlated.

15564
23:14:19,086 --> 23:14:22,320
Now moving to our fifth question in our list of interview questions.

15565
23:14:23,214 --> 23:14:26,756
The question is What is regularization in machine learning?

15566
23:14:27,120 --> 23:14:29,156
Explain the L2 regularization.

15567
23:14:30,926 --> 23:14:39,716
So regularization is a machine learning technique that is used to reduce the errors by fitting the function appropriately on the training set in order to avoid overfitting of data.

15568
23:14:40,014 --> 23:14:48,772
So overfitting happens when a model learns the detail and noise in the training data to the extent that it negatively impacts the performance of the model on new data.

15569
23:14:49,006 --> 23:14:50,468
So here you can see we have

15570
23:14:51,182 --> 23:14:55,524
a nice plot which shows how overfitting of data can be visualized.

15571
23:14:56,494 --> 23:14:57,040
And here

15572
23:14:58,094 --> 23:15:01,988
we have a good fit line over the same data points.

15573
23:15:02,446 --> 23:15:04,740
So this is also known as the regression line.

15574
23:15:05,486 --> 23:15:08,772
Now L2 regularization is also known as ridge regression.

15575
23:15:09,040 --> 23:15:16,548
So ridge regression modifies the overfit and model by adding the squared magnitude of coefficient as a penalty term to the loss function.

15576
23:15:16,942 --> 23:15:27,780
So on the right you can see a set of data points plotted and we have our linear regression line, and here we are calculating the cost function for the ridge regression line.

15577
23:15:28,846 --> 23:15:39,236
So our cost function is actually loss plus lambda into summation of W squared where loss is actually the sum of squared errors or squared residuals.

15578
23:15:39,854 --> 23:15:44,900
Lambda stands for penalty for the errors, W is called the slope of the curve or line.

15579
23:15:46,320 --> 23:15:47,780
Now consider a case

15580
23:15:48,878 --> 23:15:52,480
where there are two points passing through the linear regression

15581
23:15:52,480 --> 23:15:55,556
line. Now if you calculate the cost function,

15582
23:15:56,238 --> 23:15:58,240
we get the value as 1.69.

15583
23:15:58,606 --> 23:16:03,652
So here we have assumed that loss is 0 since the two points lie directly on the line.

15584
23:16:04,142 --> 23:16:07,556
We have taken lambda to be 1 and w is 1.3.

15585
23:16:08,014 --> 23:16:11,200
So if you use this function or this formula,

15586
23:16:11,918 --> 23:16:14,692
you get the cost function as 1.69.

15587
23:16:16,046 --> 23:16:23,492
Now moving ahead, let's consider another situation where we will calculate the same cost function for the ridge regression line.

15588
23:16:24,080 --> 23:16:28,548
There is some loss for both the points as they are not on the same line.

15589
23:16:28,942 --> 23:16:37,668
So here you can see the sum of squared residuals is 0.05, which actually is the sum of 0.2 square, I am assuming this as 0.2

15590
23:16:38,160 --> 23:16:39,812
and 0.1 for this one.

15591
23:16:41,230 --> 23:16:49,536
So, if you square both and add it, the value is 0.05, lambda is again 1, and w we have assumed to be 0.6.

15592
23:16:50,094 --> 23:16:53,412
Now, if you find the cost function, the value is 0.41.

15593
23:16:53,998 --> 23:16:58,180
Let's draw the linear regression line and the ridge regression line with all the points.

15594
23:16:58,416 --> 23:17:03,524
We find that the ridge regression line has the best fit since its cost function is less.

