23:19:00,398 --> 23:19:02,276
Now moving to the seventh question.

15616
23:19:03,982 --> 23:19:08,932
So the question is how do we find the optimum cluster value in k means clustering algorithm?

15617
23:19:09,456 --> 23:19:12,548
Now there are two methods to find the optimum cluster value.

15618
23:19:13,136 --> 23:19:19,300
So first we have the elbow method, which is one of the most well known for finding the optimum number of clusters.

15619
23:19:19,856 --> 23:19:28,336
So in this method, you need to calculate the within cluster sum of squared errors for different values of k and choose the k for which within cluster

15620
23:19:28,496 --> 23:19:31,492
sum of squared errors first starts to diminish.

15621
23:19:32,334 --> 23:19:36,132
So in the below plot of squared errors versus the number of clusters k,

15622
23:19:36,782 --> 23:19:45,316
you can see at k is equal to 4 the squared error starts to diminish, so hence our optimum k value is 4.

15623
23:19:46,190 --> 23:19:48,708
Next we have the siloid method.

15624
23:19:49,870 --> 23:19:55,748
So the siloid method measures how similar a point is to its own cluster compared to other clusters.

15625
23:19:56,366 --> 23:20:01,856
The average silloid method computes the average siloid of observations for different values of k.

15626
23:20:02,286 --> 23:20:09,316
The optimum number of clusters k is the one that maximizes the average siloid over a range of possible values for k.

15627
23:20:10,062 --> 23:20:13,796
The siloid score reaches its global maximum at the optimal k.

15628
23:20:14,094 --> 23:20:23,428
So in our case, the average silloid reaches maximum at k is equal to 2, which you can see here. So our optimum cluster value will be 2 here.

