23:20:25,870 --> 23:20:27,556
the eighth question in our list is

15631
23:20:28,206 --> 23:20:31,428
how does the pooling layer work in a convolutional neural network?

15632
23:20:32,078 --> 23:20:38,148
So the pooling layer performs a downsampling operation in order to reduce the dimensionality of the feature map.

15633
23:20:38,656 --> 23:20:48,004
So in the pooling operation, you slide a two dimensional filter over each channel of feature map and summarize the features lying within the region covered by the filter.

15634
23:20:49,550 --> 23:20:57,028
It is a common practice to periodically insert a pooling layer in between successive convolutional layers in a convolutional neural network architecture.

15635
23:20:57,998 --> 23:21:06,468
So the pooling layer operates independently on every depth slice in the input and resizes it spatially using the max operation.

15636
23:21:07,342 --> 23:21:12,292
So in the diagram shown here, you can see we have a rectified feature map.

15637
23:21:12,750 --> 23:21:17,796
We are using a 2 cross 2 filter and performing a max pooling operation.

15638
23:21:18,478 --> 23:21:23,652
So consider this as the filter if you perform the max operation over the values,

15639
23:21:25,006 --> 23:21:32,260
let us say 0, 5, 3, and 1. So considering this one, our pool feature map maximum value will be 5.

15640
23:21:32,526 --> 23:21:35,908
Similarly, for this chunk of data, it is going to be 7.

15641
23:21:36,270 --> 23:21:42,336
Next, if you slide the filter over this square frame, you get 8, and similarly, here you get 6.

15642
23:21:42,496 --> 23:21:45,220
So this is also known as a pooled feature map.

15643
23:21:46,094 --> 23:21:46,852
