15644
23:21:47,790 --> 23:21:51,296
the ninth question in our list is how does LSTM network work?

15645
23:21:51,694 --> 23:21:58,916
So long short-term memory networks are a type of recurrent neural networks that are capable of learning audit dependence and sequence prediction problems.

15646
23:21:59,216 --> 23:22:03,748
So remembering information for long periods of time is practically their default behavior.

15647
23:22:04,302 --> 23:22:08,900
Now LSTMs also have this chain-like structure which you can see here,

15648
23:22:10,286 --> 23:22:12,676
but the repeating module has a different structure.

15649
23:22:13,102 --> 23:22:19,172
So instead of having a single neural network layer, there are four interacting in a very special way.

15650
23:22:19,630 --> 23:22:21,856
Now you can see these are called as gates.

15651
23:22:22,030 --> 23:22:24,260
These gates contain sigmoid activations.

15652
23:22:24,846 --> 23:22:28,036
A sigmoid activation is similar to the tan H activation.

15653
23:22:28,496 --> 23:22:34,884
Instead of squishing values between minus 1 and plus 1, it squishes values between 0 and 1.

15654
23:22:35,790 --> 23:22:37,572
An LSTM has four gates.

15655
23:22:39,950 --> 23:22:44,164
Now these are called forget, remember, learn and use or output.

15656
23:22:44,526 --> 23:22:51,652
So if you see this, in the first step, we use the forget gate that decides what information should be thrown away or kept.

15657
23:22:52,176 --> 23:22:58,596
The information from the previous hidden state and the information from the current input is passed through the sigmoid function.

15658
23:22:59,054 --> 23:23:01,092
Values come out between 0 and 1.

15659
23:23:02,446 --> 23:23:06,148
So if the value is closer to 0, it means you need to forget that information.

15660
23:23:06,574 --> 23:23:10,532
And if the value is closer to 1, it means you need to keep that information.

15661
23:23:11,662 --> 23:23:13,936
Next, we have the input gate.

15662
23:23:14,126 --> 23:23:16,804
So the input gate is used to update the cell state.

15663
23:23:17,056 --> 23:23:21,956
First, we pass the previous hidden state and the current input into a sigmoid function.

15664
23:23:23,056 --> 23:23:28,496
That decides which values will be updated by transforming the values to be between 0 and 1.

15665
23:23:28,846 --> 23:23:31,696
0 means not important and 1 means important.

15666
23:23:32,590 --> 23:23:39,136
You also pass the hidden state and current input into the Tanage function to flatten the values between minus 1 and plus 1.

15667
23:23:39,502 --> 23:23:41,156
This helps to regulate the network.

15668
23:23:42,350 --> 23:23:45,796
Then you multiply the Tanage output with the sigmoid output.

15669
23:23:46,222 --> 23:23:50,948
The sigmoid output will decide which information is important to keep from the Tanage output.

15670
23:23:52,302 --> 23:23:55,396
And finally in step 3, we have the output gate.

15671
23:23:55,696 --> 23:23:57,732
This output gate is used to decide

15672
23:23:58,446 --> 23:24:00,420
what the next hidden state should be.

15673
23:24:00,816 --> 23:24:05,508
First, we pass the previous hidden state and the current input into a sigmoid function.

15674
23:24:05,936 --> 23:24:10,244
Then we pass the newly modified cell state into the Tanage function.

15675
23:24:11,214 --> 23:24:17,060
We then multiply the TANH output with the sigmoid output to decide what information the hidden state should carry.

15676
23:24:17,536 --> 23:24:19,396
The output is the hidden state.

15677
23:24:19,886 --> 23:24:24,740
The new cell state and the new hidden state is then carried over to the next time step.

