24:06:07,374 --> 24:06:12,548
So you might be wondering what's changed in machine learning and why is it the best time to get into it now?

16096
24:06:12,960 --> 24:06:19,492
Well, let's go back a few years. In the past, machine learning was more about building models based on historical data.

16097
24:06:19,760 --> 24:06:27,920
It was about training algorithms to predict specific outcomes like classifying emails, spam or not spam, predicting house prices based on past data.

16098
24:06:28,080 --> 24:06:32,320
But fast forward to 2026 and the landscape has changed dramatically.

16099
24:06:32,494 --> 24:06:38,880
Today, machine learning isn't just about making predictions, it's about building systems that can learn, adapt, and improve over time.

16100
24:06:39,120 --> 24:06:42,640
We're no longer creating algorithms to just run experiments offline.

16101
24:06:42,800 --> 24:06:50,820
Now, machine learning systems are integrated into real-world operations and are capable of making decisions that impact the business immediately.

16102
24:06:51,120 --> 24:07:00,320
Here's an example to make it clearer. In the past, an e-commerce website might use machine learning to predict what products a customer might want based on their past purchases.

16103
24:07:00,480 --> 24:07:09,092
Now, the systems can constantly learn from new customer data, continuously refining those predictions in real time as customers' preferences are changing.

16104
24:07:09,440 --> 24:07:16,000
The world of machine learning has evolved from theory to practice, and this has created a huge demand for machine learning engineers

16105
24:07:16,160 --> 24:07:20,080
who can build scalable systems and make them work in real world environments.

16106
24:07:20,240 --> 24:07:27,200
The impact of machine learning is now directly tied to business outcomes, and machine learning engineers are at the center of that transformation.

16107
24:07:27,440 --> 24:07:35,520
You might be thinking, okay, I get it, machine learning is impactful, but what exactly does a machine learning engineer do compared to other roles in tech?

16108
24:07:35,680 --> 24:07:36,720
That's a great question.

16109
24:07:36,880 --> 24:07:46,820
In the world of machine learning, you'll hear about a few key roles such as data scientist, machine learning, and AI engineer. Let's break them down so you know exactly where you fit in.

16110
24:07:47,120 --> 24:07:49,764
Data scientists are like the detectives of data.

16111
24:07:50,000 --> 24:07:56,000
They spend their time analyzing large data sets, finding trends, and trying to extract meaningful insights.

16112
24:07:56,240 --> 24:08:06,800
They build models, but their main focus is usually on data exploration and experimenting with various algorithms. They don't typically focus on deploying those models into production environments.

16113
24:08:06,960 --> 24:08:15,680
Machine learning engineers. On the other hand, these are architects. They take the models built by data scientists and with scalable, deployable systems.

16114
24:08:15,840 --> 24:08:23,200
They work on creating solutions that will not only work in the short term, but can also scale to handle real-world data in massive volumes.

16115
24:08:23,360 --> 24:08:32,160
The machine learning engineer is responsible for ensuring that machine learning systems are integrated into businesses that can work seamlessly with existing technologies.

16116
24:08:32,334 --> 24:08:35,600
AI engineers focus more on the application side of things.

16117
24:08:35,854 --> 24:08:40,720
They build AI-powered products like chatbots, voice assistants, and real-time systems.

16118
24:08:40,880 --> 24:08:48,560
While their work often overlaps with ML engineers, they are typically more focused on the user-facing product and how machine learning fits into it.

16119
24:08:48,720 --> 24:08:56,260
As an ML engineer, your primary focus is to take models and turn them into actionable solutions that are deployed in real-world systems.

