17863
27:01:38,224 --> 27:01:46,544
Statistics finds relationships through methods like regression and correlation, revealing how variables impact each other.

17864
27:01:46,704 --> 27:01:54,064
And finally, all these tools enable data-driven decision making, turning raw numbers into actionable insights.

17865
27:01:54,318 --> 27:01:57,984
Now let's talk about why does statistics matter in data science?

17866
27:01:58,224 --> 27:02:07,264
Statistics form the backbone of data science, providing the mathematical framework needed to make sense of data and draw reliable conclusions.

17867
27:02:07,504 --> 27:02:15,908
Without statistics, it would be impossible to turn raw data into meaningful insights or make confident evidence-based decisions.

17868
27:02:16,224 --> 27:02:20,064
Now let's have a look at the main branches of statistics.

17869
27:02:20,384 --> 27:02:28,624
Descriptive statistics and inferential statistics. So first let's talk about the definition. Then we have got methods and measures.

17870
27:02:28,704 --> 27:02:40,772
Now in the case of descriptive statistics, now let's have a look at the main branches of statistics. So basically there are two core branches: descriptive statistics and inferential statistics.

17871
27:02:41,024 --> 27:02:49,424
Descriptive statistics summarizes and describes data using measures like mean, median, mode, range, and standard deviation.

17872
27:02:49,664 --> 27:02:56,036
Its purpose is to organize and present data typically with charts, graph or summary tables.

17873
27:02:56,304 --> 27:03:01,024
The scope of descriptive statistics is limited to the sample data itself.

17874
27:03:01,344 --> 27:03:07,984
On the other hand, inferential statistics makes inferences about populations based on samples.

17875
27:03:08,144 --> 27:03:13,764
It uses methods like hypothesis testing, confidence intervals, and regression.

17876
27:03:14,064 --> 27:03:22,756
The purpose here is to draw conclusions and make predictions with common examples including A B testing and survey analysis.

17877
27:03:23,104 --> 27:03:37,184
The scope of inferential statistics extends beyond the sample to the larger population. Understanding both these branches is essential for analyzing and interpreting data in any data science project.

17878
27:03:37,344 --> 27:03:43,824
Now let's take a closer look at the descriptive statistics, starting with measures of central tendency.

17879
27:03:43,984 --> 27:03:53,984
The first measure here is mean, which is the average of all the values simply calculated as the sum of all the data points divided by the total count.

17880
27:03:54,224 --> 27:04:04,932
Next is the median, which represents the middle value when the data is sorted from lowest to highest. This is especially useful when dealing with skewed distributions.

17881
27:04:05,184 --> 27:04:14,464
And finally, the mode is the most frequently occurring value in the data set, helping us identify common patterns or repeated outcomes.

17882
27:04:14,704 --> 27:04:21,024
Let's continue our deep dive into descriptive statistics by looking at the measures of variability.

17883
27:04:21,294 --> 27:04:22,944
First, we've got the range.

17884
27:04:23,214 --> 27:04:34,624
This is simply the difference between the maximum and minimum value in a dataset, showing us the spread of our data, which measures the average of the square differences from the mean.

17885
27:04:35,024 --> 27:04:39,984
This tells us how much the values in our data set differ from the average.

17886
27:04:40,224 --> 27:04:52,304
Closely related is the standard deviation, which is the square root of the variance. It gives us a more intuitive sense of how much the values typically deviate from the mean.

17887
27:04:52,704 --> 27:04:57,024
And finally, we've got the interquartile range, or we say IQR.

17888
27:04:57,504 --> 27:05:08,784
This shows us the range of the middle 50% of our data, helping us understand how data is distributed across the center and avoid the effect of outliers.

17889
27:05:09,024 --> 27:05:18,564
Understanding these four measures allows us to summarize not just the center of our data but how spread out and varied our data set is.

17890
27:05:18,864 --> 27:05:23,424
Now let's look at some practical applications of descriptive statistics.

17891
27:05:23,664 --> 27:05:32,944
One major use is the data exploration and summarization, where we quickly get an overview and basic understanding of complex data sets,

17892
27:05:33,104 --> 27:05:38,944
helping track performance and detect problems early in fields like manufacturing or operations.

17893
27:05:39,264 --> 27:05:50,704
