22768
33:09:18,030 --> 33:09:21,392
Support vector machine is a widely used classification algorithm.

22769
33:09:21,614 --> 33:09:23,712
The idea of support vector machine is simple.

22770
33:09:23,872 --> 33:09:28,352
The algorithm creates a separation line which divides the classes in the best possible manner.

22771
33:09:28,592 --> 33:09:31,792
For example, dog or cat, disease or no disease.

22772
33:09:32,046 --> 33:09:37,712
Suppose we have a labeled sample data which tells height and weight of males and females.

22773
33:09:38,222 --> 33:09:42,192
A new data point arrives and we want to know whether it's going to be a male or a female.

22774
33:09:42,352 --> 33:09:44,512
So we start by drawing a line.

22775
33:09:44,750 --> 33:09:50,628
We draw decision lines, but if we consider decision line one, then we will classify the individual as a male.

22776
33:09:50,862 --> 33:09:54,352
And if we consider decision line two, then it will be a female.

22777
33:09:54,670 --> 33:09:59,952
So you can see this person kind of lies in the middle of the two groups, so it's a little confusing trying to figure out which line they should be under.

22778
33:10:00,142 --> 33:10:03,872
We need to know which line divides the classes correctly, but how.

22779
33:10:04,302 --> 33:10:10,992
The goal is to choose a hyperplane, and that is one of the key words they use when we talk about support vector machines.

22780
33:10:11,342 --> 33:10:18,032
Choose a hyperplane with the greatest possible margin between the decision line and the nearest point within the training set.

22781
33:10:18,382 --> 33:10:25,392
So you can see here we have our support vector, we have the two nearest points to it, and we draw a line between those two points.

22782
33:10:25,646 --> 33:10:31,552
And the distance margin is the distance between the hyperplane and the nearest data point from either set.

22783
33:10:31,854 --> 33:10:38,272
So we actually have a value, and it should be equally distant between the two points that we're comparing it to.

22784
33:10:38,510 --> 33:10:48,832
When we draw the hyperplanes, we observe that line one has a maximum distance. So we observe that line one has a maximum distance margin, so we'll classify the new data point correctly.

22785
33:10:49,102 --> 33:10:52,752
And our result on this one is going to be that the new data point is MEL.

22786
33:10:53,072 --> 33:11:01,312
One of the reasons we call it a hyperplane versus a line is that a lot of times we're not looking at just weight and height.

22787
33:11:01,486 --> 33:11:05,232
We might be looking at thirty-six different features or dimensions.

22788
33:11:05,486 --> 33:11:13,072
And so when we cut it with a hyperplane, it's more of a three-dimensional cut in the data or multi-dimensional. It cuts the data a certain way.

22789
33:11:13,232 --> 33:11:17,872
And each plane continues to cut it down until we get the best fit or match.

