45:28:33,102 --> 45:28:34,128
So how does this work?

30723
45:28:34,350 --> 45:28:41,968
The way it works is the first step in k-means clustering is the allocation of two centroids randomly.

30724
45:28:42,128 --> 45:28:52,608
So two points are assigned as so-called centroids. So in this case we want two clusters, which means k is equal to two.

30725
45:28:52,928 --> 45:29:06,724
So two points have been randomly assigned as centroids. Keep in mind these points can be anywhere, they are random points. They are not initially, they are not really the centroids.

30726
45:29:07,246 --> 45:29:15,008
Centroid means it's a central point of a given data set, but in this case, when it starts off, it's not really the centroid.

30727
45:29:15,168 --> 45:29:26,756
Okay, so these points, though in our presentation here we have shown them one point closer to these data points and another closer to these data points, they can be assigned randomly anywhere.

30728
45:29:26,928 --> 45:29:28,768
Okay, so that's the first step.

30729
45:29:28,928 --> 45:29:38,288
The next step is to determine the distance of each of the data points from each of the randomly assigned centroids.

30730
45:29:38,448 --> 45:29:45,488
So for example, we take this point and find the distance from this centroid and the distance from this centroid.

30731
45:29:45,648 --> 45:29:50,528
This point is taken and the distance is found from this centroid and this centroid, and so on and so forth.

30732
45:29:51,376 --> 45:29:59,856
For every point, the distance is measured from both the centroids, and then whichever distance is less,

30733
45:30:00,096 --> 45:30:09,936
that point is assigned to that centroid. So, for example, in this case, visually it is very obvious that all these data points are assigned to this centroid,

30734
45:30:10,096 --> 45:30:17,056
and all these data points are assigned to this centroid, and that's what is represented here in blue color and in this yellow color.

30735
45:30:17,216 --> 45:30:25,056
The next step is to actually determine the central point or the actual centroid for these two clusters.

30736
45:30:25,230 --> 45:30:32,576
So we have this one initial cluster, this one initial cluster, but as you can see, these points are not really the centroid.

30737
45:30:32,736 --> 45:30:38,656
Centroid means it should be the central position of this data set, central position of this data set.

30738
45:30:38,816 --> 45:30:42,756
So that is what needs to be determined as the next step.

30739
45:30:43,214 --> 45:30:54,816
So the central point or the actual centroid is determined, and the original randomly allocated centroid is repositioned to the actual centroid of this new clusters.

30740
45:30:54,976 --> 45:31:02,416
And this process is actually repeated. Now what might happen is some of these points may get reallocated.

30741
45:31:02,576 --> 45:31:11,748
In our example, that is not happening probably, but it may so happen that the distance is found between each of these data points once again with these centroids.

30742
45:31:12,016 --> 45:31:20,004
And if there is if it is required, some points may be reallocated. We will see that in a later example, but for now we will keep it simple.

30743
45:31:20,336 --> 45:31:29,056
So this process is continued till the centroid repositioning stops, and that is our final cluster.

30744
45:31:29,296 --> 45:31:37,924
So this is our so after iteration, we come to this position, this situation where the centroid doesn't need any more repositioning,

30745
45:31:38,096 --> 45:31:41,776
and that means our algorithm has converged.

30746
45:31:41,998 --> 45:31:48,484
Convergence has occurred, and we have the cluster, two clusters, we have the clusters with a centroid.

30747
45:31:48,656 --> 45:31:51,376
So this process is repeated.

30748
45:31:51,696 --> 45:32:00,256
The process of calculating the distance and repositioning the centroid is repeated till the repositioning stops,

30749
45:32:00,416 --> 45:32:09,060
which means that the algorithm has converged and we have the final cluster with the data points and the centroids.

